///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
//  OPERATION MODES DEMONSTRATION
//
//  This program demonstrates the two operational modes of the Cortex-M4 processor:
//
//  1. THREAD MODE  - normal code execution (main, your functions, your loops)
//  2. HANDLER MODE - interrupt/exception handling (ISR functions)
//
//  The flow of this program:
//
//    Processor resets
//      |
//      Startup code runs (Thread mode)
//      |
//      main() is called (Thread mode)
//        |
//        printf("before interrupt")
//        |
//        calls generate_interrupt()          <-- still Thread mode
//          |
//          enables IRQ3
//          triggers IRQ3 software interrupt  <-- processor SWITCHES to Handler mode
//            |
//            RTC_WKUP_IRQHandler() runs      <-- Handler mode (full privileged access)
//            printf("ISR")
//            handler finishes                <-- processor SWITCHES BACK to Thread mode
//          |
//        printf("after interrupt")           <-- back in Thread mode, exactly where we left off
//        |
//        for(;;) loops forever
//
//  KEY TAKEAWAYS:
//    - After reset, the processor ALWAYS starts in Thread mode
//    - The ONLY way to enter Handler mode is through an exception or interrupt
//    - When the handler finishes, the processor automatically returns to Thread mode
//    - Handler mode ALWAYS has privileged access (full control of everything)
//    - Thread mode has privileged access by default, but CAN be restricted
//
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

